<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Decolonisation</h1><p class="path-tagline">How Asia and Africa won their independence in the thirty years after 1945.</p><p class="path-intro">In 1945 most of Africa and much of Asia was still ruled from Europe. Thirty years later the colonial empires were almost gone. Some colonies won independence by negotiation and some by war; the new states met at Bandung to speak with their own voice, while the old powers learned at Suez and in Algeria that they could no longer hold on by force. This path follows that end of empire from the partition of India to the Portuguese revolution of 1974, through its leaders, crises and speeches.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/decolonisation.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Event</span><span class="path-step-name">Decolonisation</span><span class="path-step-why">Colonies across Asia and Africa won independence in the decades after the Second World War, ending the British and French empires as global systems.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india"><span class="path-step-ph">2</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1947</span><span class="path-step-name">Partition of India</span><span class="path-step-why">British India became India and Pakistan in August 1947; some fifteen million people crossed the new borders amid communal killing.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bandung-conference.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1955</span><span class="path-step-name">Bandung Conference</span><span class="path-step-why">In 1955 twenty-nine newly independent and emerging states of Asia and Africa met in Indonesia and condemned colonialism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suez-crisis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1956</span><span class="path-step-name">Suez Crisis</span><span class="path-step-why">Britain, France and Israel invaded Egypt after Nasser nationalised the canal in 1956, and withdrew under American and Soviet pressure.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1909–1972</span><span class="path-step-name">Kwame Nkrumah</span><span class="path-step-why">He led the Gold Coast to independence in 1957 and named the new state Ghana, after the medieval empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/wind-of-change-speech"><span class="path-step-ph">6</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1960</span><span class="path-step-name">Wind of Change speech</span><span class="path-step-why">In Cape Town in 1960 Harold Macmillan acknowledged the strength of African national consciousness and signalled that Britain would not resist decolonisation.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1925–1961</span><span class="path-step-name">Patrice Lumumba</span><span class="path-step-why">The Congo's first prime minister in June 1960, deposed that September and killed in January 1961 with Belgian complicity.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/algerian-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Event · ended 1962</span><span class="path-step-name">Algerian War</span><span class="path-step-why">Eight years of war, from 1954 to 1962, ended French rule in Algeria and brought a political crisis in France itself.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1974</span><span class="path-step-name">Carnation Revolution</span><span class="path-step-why">A coup in Lisbon in April 1974, driven partly by the costly colonial wars, brought the Portuguese Empire in Africa to an end.</span></span></a></li></ol>
